A `Chunk` stores its component columns as `ListArray`s, where each entry in the list-array is a component batch.
```rust
pub struct Chunk {
    // ...
    pub(crate) components: BTreeMap<ComponentName, ArrowListArray<i32>>,
    // ...
}
```

For most cases this is fine, but for small mono-components this is not optimal as we have to spend extra compute and space on list offsets that we don't need.

The worst case, as usual, is `Scalar`s: with a `ListArray<i32>` that's a 50% space overhead we have pay in order to store a... monotonically increasing list of offsets. :unamused:

We are actually not that far from being able to support component columns that are backed directly by their underlying `list.values()` array instead of the list itself, for these cases.
This would allow e.g. the Python SDK to pretty much pass a numpy scalar array as-is, without any `array_to_list_array()` shenanigans.

In effect this means moving to something like this:
```rust
pub struct Chunk {
    // ...
    pub(crate) components: BTreeMap<ComponentName, Either<ArrowListArray<i32>, Box<dyn ArrowArray>>>,
    // ...
}
```
which is really the same as:
```rust
pub struct Chunk {
    // ...
    pub(crate) components: BTreeMap<ComponentName, Box<dyn ArrowArray>>,
    // ...
}
```

There are a few subtleties to get right though, especially when it comes to all the sorting/shuffling/slicing we do, where we basically need to have specialized implementations for `ListArray` vs. everything else.
Nothing unsurmountable though, ish?

(Might also cause issues with `RecordBatch`es and stuff? Can't see why, but I haven't run any tests really)

(Also there's the whole business of filling up buffers with null values across columns etc)

